to improve device performance, only a limited number of groups are investigating Sn-based PSC at present. The difficulty in fabricating high-quality Sn perovskite films is a significant impediment.[15–19]
In this paper we focus on the development of reliable procedures for fabricating uniform, continuous, 'pinhole free' Sn perovskite films with large grain size and good electrical properties – desirable traits which can be expected to lead to efficient and reproducible Sn-based PSCs. Techniques to optimize the formation of nucleation sites and control the crystal growth process are introduced. With the first technique, which we call 'hot antisolvent treatment' (HAT), antisolvent is pre-heated to improve the film coverage during spin coating, eliminating the large pinholes observed when the antisolvent is used at room temperature. With the second technique, based on solvent vapor annealing (SVA), improved film uniformity and increased crystal grain size is achieved by annealing under a low partial pressure dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO) vapor environment. Combined, a simple and reliable protocol for fabricating efficient Sn-based PSCs is established, with the best device reaching a PCE of \(7.2\%\). Although \(\mathsf{F A S n l}_{3}\) is commonly used as the Sn perovskite absorber layer,[16–23] we have demonstrated that alignment between the valence band (VB) of \(\mathsf{F A S n l}_{3}\) perovskite and the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) level of commonly used hole-transporting materials (HTM: PEDOT:PSS, SpiroOMeTAD, etc.) is poor.[27] The mixed-organic cation \(\mathsf{F A}_{x}\mathsf{M A}_{1-x}\mathsf{S n l}_{3}\) perovskite recently proposed by Zhao et al[17] is therefore used in the present study. Using photoelectron yield spectroscopy (PYS) under vacuum (Figure S1a), photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy under inert atmosphere (Figure S1b) and scanning electron microscope (SEM) images (Figure S1d-h), the \(\mathsf{F A}_{0.75}\mathsf{M A}_{0.25}\mathsf{S n l}_{3}\) perovskite film was confirmed to have a more suitable valence band (VB) position as well as relatively homogeneous film morphology. The one-step solution processed \(\mathsf{F A}_{0.75}\mathsf{M A}_{0.25}\mathsf{S n l}_{3}\) perovskite was used to fabricate inverted solar cells shown in Figure 1. PEDOT:PSS and \(\mathsf{C}_{60}\) were used as the hole and electron collection layers, respectively. PEDOT:PSS was chosen over SpiroOMeTAD because of the more suitable alignment of the HOMO energy level with the valence band of Sn-based perovskites. \(\mathsf{C}_{60}\) was selected for the electron transport layer principally for the morphological advantages: Vacuum deposited \(\mathsf{C}_{60}\) evenly and fully coats the rough perovskite films. A thin layer of bathocuproine (BCP) was deposited between \(\mathsf{C}_{60}\) and silver electrode to facilitate electron transfer. The relevant energy levels of each material are also shown.
## COMMUNICATION
Initially, devices were fabricated following the reported procedures exactly.[17] A solution of \(\mathsf{F A}_{0.75}\mathsf{M A}_{0.25}\mathsf{S n l}_{3}\) in DMSO was used as the precursor and the spin coating was performed at 5000 rpm for \(100~\mathfrak{s}\). The chlorobenzene (Ph-Cl) antisolvent was dropped onto the spinning film at the 63 s mark to promote faster crystallization and the perovskite film was afterwards annealed at \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The entire process was performed under Ar atmosphere. Details of the method can be found in the supporting information. \(10\;\mathrm{mol}\%\) of \(\mathsf{S n F}_{2}\) was added in the precursor to stabilize the Sn perovskite and to prevent oxidation.[14] The film color turned from transparent to dark brown immediately after antisolvent dripping, but faded to a transparent film again before the spinning was stopped at 100 s (Figure S2a). After annealing the film is noticeably rough. This is very different from Pb-based perovskite fabrication, where the Pb intermediate states are transparent or show light color.[28–34] With the stable Pb intermediate states serving as nucleation centers, crystal growth is retarded under room temperature, enabling a more controllable film formation process.[29–34] Instead, for Sn-based perovskite, we suggest that the small perovskite crystals serving as nucleation centers form immediately after antisolvent dripping. The proposed mechanism is shown in Figure 2. The absence of an intermediate crystalline state is confirmed by the XRD pattern, which is similar to that of the completed film of \(\mathsf{F A}_{0.75}\mathsf{M A}_{0.25}\mathsf{S n l}_{3}\) (Figure S3a). The Sn perovskite crystal nucleation centers are unstable at room temperature and can be easily dissolved by DMSO vapor when the ratio of antisolvent in the substrate is reduced, causing the film to fade and making it difficult to control film morphology.[15,16] We therefore shortened the spin coating time to 66 s (i.e. leaving just 3 s after antisolvent dripping) and performed the thermal annealing immediately after spin coating. A much smoother film was obtained (Figure S2b). This modified spin coating procedure was adopted for fabricating the reference films and all the devices used in the present study.
As shown in the top view SEM image in Figure 2c, \(\mathsf{F A}_{0.75}\mathsf{M A}_{0.25}\mathsf{S n l}_{3}\) film prepared with room temperature (r.t.) \((<\!25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) Ph-Cl still shows imperfect coverage. There are a large number of voids with sizes ranging from several hundred nanometres up to around 2 micrometres distributed randomly among the crystal grains. These large pinholes' allow shunting pathways to form between the charge extraction layers, reducing the PCE. The formation of the pinholes is ascribed to the relatively low number density of perovskite crystallites that form after dripping r.t. Ph-Cl and which anchor the subsequent film growth (Figure 2a). Increasing the number density of the Sn perovskite
nucleation sites is therefore likely to be an important requirement for growing high quality pinhole free films (Figure 2b).
Based on the observation and analysis above, we now introduce the new methods for obtaining high quality Sn perovskite films. Since it is well known that the miscibility between two solvents can be affected by temperature, and that the solvent miscibility also influences the effectiveness of antisolvent dripping,[16] the influence of the antisolvent temperature on the number density was examined in detail. Films were prepared with Ph-Cl at r.t. and four different elevated temperatures for the HAT process, 45, 65, 85 and \(100\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The SLM images suggest that while the film coverage has already improved at \(45\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) HAT small pinholes still exist (Figure S4b). Essentially \(100\%\) coverage perovskite films are obtained for Ph-Cl temperatures above \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Figure S4c-e). \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) HAT is therefore chosen as the optimal condition. The top view SEM image (Figure 2d) shows that the film is pinhole free and the individual crystallites are a few hundred nanometres in diameter. Full coverage films could also be prepared with spin coating speeds varying between 2000–5000 rpm, confirming the general utility of the HAT method (Figure S5). In all cases the preheated antisolvent facilitates the formation of a much larger number density of perovskite nucleation sites, leading to the formation of a full coverage film after annealing.
Time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) measurements are shown in Figure 3a. The decay lifetime of the perovskite film prepared with \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) HAT (3.9 ns) is longer than that of the reference film prepared with r.t. Ph-Cl (3.1 ns). This result suggests the HAT process leads to improved crystallinity and lower trap site density in the perovskite film.
A solar cell prepared with \(65\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) HAT shows superior photovoltaic performance (Figure 3b) compared with the device
