using r.t. Ph-Cl. \(\mathsf{J}_{\mathsf{S C}}\) increases from 15.6 to \(18.8\ m A{\cdot}c m^{-2}\) , opencircuit voltage \((V_{\infty})\) increases from 0.36 to \(0.51~\mathrm{~V~}\) , and the fill factor (FF) increases from 0.52 to 0.69. The overall PCE increases from 3.0 to \(6.6\%\) . All the cells show negligible hysteresis in their \(J{-}V\) curves (Figure S6). Further evidence that the electrical shunting path is effectively reduced using HAT is provided by the shunt resistance \((R_{\mathsf{S h}})\) , which increases from 41 to \(480\ \Omega\ c m^{-2}\) . The solar cell performances in the cells with 85 and \(100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) decrease drastically (Figure S6) even while the film coverage remains high (Figure S4d-e). It may be that the high temperature solvent damaged the PEDOT:PSS layer.[35-38] Clearly \(65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) HAT is the optimal condition when fabricating solar cells with the inverted structure containing PEDOT:PSS.
After adopting \(65~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) HAT as the standard procedure for perovskite film formation, we next optimize the process of crystal growth during the annealing step. The rapid crystal growth of Sn perovskite during thermal annealing can result in the formation of a large number of defects in the final film which may limit the free carrier diffusion length.[11,16] Several strategies for retarding the crystal growth rate of Pb perovskite during annealing, such as ionic liquid addition[37], hot-casting method[38], and solvent annealing[39-42] have been reported. All are aimed at reducing the defect density in the film. As has been previously mentioned, small perovskite crystallites can be easily dissolved by the strong DMSO vapor. A mild DMSO vapor-enriched atmosphere during annealing might however influence the crystal growth without damaging the perovskite film outright. To test this speculation, several variants of DMSO treatment were developed, illustrated schematically in Figure 4a. In method 1, the film was covered with a petri dish. A mild DMSO atmosphere is created inside the petri dish as DMSO is released from the film during thermal annealing. In method 2 and 3, one small drop of DMSO \((<1,0\ \upmu\mathsf{L})\) was added to a plastic cap inside the petri dish to further increase the vapor density of DMSO. To avoid perovskite crystals from being dissolved by the high concentrations of DMSO vapor, the additional DMSO was only presented for the first \(10\,\mathfrak{s}\) (method 2) or \(30\,\textbf{s}\) (method 3) of the thermal annealing, after which the sample was returned to the petri dish of method 1. A photo of the experimental setup is shown in Figure S7. Figure 4b-e shows how the homogeneity of the size and shape of crystal grain improves with the DMSO vapor treatments compared to the untreated film. The average grain size increases from \(237\;\mathsf{n m}\) for the reference to \(328~\mathsf{n m}\) for method 1, \(394~\mathsf{n m}\) for method 2, and \(440~\mathsf{n m}\) for method 3. Fewer crystal grain boundaries in the film is a desirable
outcome as the associated trap sites at grain boundaries can lead to additional charge carrier recombination.
The \(J{-}V\) curves of our best-performing treated devices are compared in Figure 5a against the best untreated reference device obtained from the same fabrication batch. All the solar cells show negligible hysteresis in their \(J{-}V\) curves (Figure S8). The solar cell performance parameters are listed in Table 1. With the DMSO vapor treatment, the \(\mathsf{J}_{\mathsf{S C}}\) increases to over \(20\,\mathsf{\ m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}\) compared with that of \(17.2\,\mathsf{\ m A}/\mathsf{c m}^{2}\) for the untreated anneal condition. As a result, the overall PCE improves from 5.7 to around \(7.0\%\) for treated devices, with the best device showing a PCE of \(7.2\%\) . The PL decay lifetimes are shown in Figure S9. Although the PL lifetimes are largely unchanged, the increase in device performance is in good accordance with the changes in film morphology.
Figure 5b shows the stabilized power output (SPO) and current density of the best performing device over time recorded at a fixed applied voltage of \(0.42\,\mathrm{V}\) . The SPO of \(6.5\%\) is observed after 100 s measurement. From the statistics of 39 devices fabricated with HAT and SVA compiled in Figure 5c and Figure S10, the average PCE is \(6.47\%\) and the standard deviation is found to be \(0.38\%\) . These results confirm the reliability and reproducibility of the fabrication methods.
## COMMUNICATION
Quantum efficiencies were recorded in air. A typical measurement for an encapsulated device fabricated with method 1 is shown in Figure 5d. The integrated \( J_{\mathrm{SC}} \) from the external quantum efficiency (EQE) was estimated to be \( 18.3\,\mathrm{mA}/\mathrm{cm}^{2} \), comparable with the values obtained for the unencapsulated cells (Table 1). The current onset occurs at about \( 520\,\mathrm{nm} \) (1.35 eV), in close agreement with the optical gap (1.36 eV, Figure S1). The peak internal quantum efficiency (IQE) is \( 74.0\% \) at \( 580\,\mathrm{nm} \) and the peak EQE is \( 66.4\% \) at \( 540\,\mathrm{nm} \). The moderate quantum efficiencies suggest that non-radiative recombination caused by defect states and impurities is still significant. Further studies are required to increase the purity of the materials[27] and improve the fabrication techniques for Sn-based perovskite devices with fewer defects. This work is currently in progress in our laboratory, and results will be reported in due course.
The stability of the fabricated devices was tested by placing a unencapsulated cell under either inert \( \mathsf{N}_{2} \) filled glovebox, \( \mathsf{O}_{2} < 0.1\,\mathsf{p p m} \) or ambient atmosphere and tracking the change of PCE over time. As shown in Figure S11, the device efficiency dropped drastically within 1 hour when exposed to air. Under inert condition, however, the cell was observed to retain \( 100\% \) of its initial PCE for over 70 hours. Our HAT/SVA fabrication methods yielded no discernible improvement to device stability; encapsulation is still necessary for protecting Sn perovskite from oxidation in air.
In summary, we have presented two techniques to make high quality Sn perovskite films for solar cell fabrication. By hot Ph-Cl antisolvent treatment (HAT), the number density of nucleation sites in the film is increased before thermal annealing, improving the coverage of the annealed film. The quality of the film can be further improved by introducing a low partial pressure of DMSO vapor to moderate the crystal growth rate during the thermal annealing process. The film uniformity is improved as the homogeneity and average size of crystal grain increases. Combining these two approaches, we can successfully and reproducibly fabricate highly uniform Sn perovskite films for efficient Pb-free PSCs. We expect that these simple methods for making high quality Sn perovskite film will be helpful for other research groups to overcome difficulties in related research.
